                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_rigorous:      `<strong>That’s the honest baseline.</strong> Tracing every euro to a named 2030 capability — and reclassifying the well-liked but unaligned workshops as unaligned — is what makes the heat map credible. A low alignment score you can defend beats a flattering one you can’t.`,
                    coach_r1_generous:      `<strong>A generous baseline only hides the gap from yourself.</strong> Counting popular leadership workshops as “partially aligned” makes the number look better today and leaves EUR 9.6M of inertia spend untouched. The CFO already has the audit — a softer score just looks like you’re defending the same misallocation.`,
                    coach_r1_completion:    `<strong>Completions and satisfaction are not strategy.</strong> Ranking the catalog by attendance tells you what’s popular, not what builds a 2030 capability. The most-attended course in the catalog maps to zero priorities — that’s exactly the trap the audit caught.`,
                    coach_r1_defer:         `<strong>“Too big to map in 90 days” is the answer the last L&D head gave.</strong> Deferring the diagnosis means presenting last year’s misallocation as your plan. The Board gave you 90 days precisely so you’d do this work now.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You traced the spend rigorously AND named the real problem — EUR 9.6M of inertia spend, not “training is too expensive.” That heat map gives the CFO something to act on and gives you the mandate to reallocate.`,
                    coach_r1_cost:          `<strong>Cost per learner is a symptom, not the disease.</strong> The €1.180 classroom rate is high, but the real problem is that EUR 9.6M builds no 2030 capability at any price. Fix the alignment first; the mix fixes the cost.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>“The audit overstated it” is exactly what triggered the freeze.</strong> Telling the Board the catalog is healthy when 66% of spend traces to nothing is how the function gets folded into shared services. Name the EUR 9.6M.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigor:      `<strong>A heat map with no evidence behind it won’t survive the CFO.</strong> If you can’t show how each euro was classified, the alignment score is just an assertion — and the audit already beat you to an evidenced one.`,
                    coach_r2_protect:       `<strong>That’s mode-to-objective fit.</strong> Keeping coached delivery for consultative selling and leadership while digitizing the scalable foundational content captures reach <em>and</em> protects the relational learning objective. Reach that changes behavior is the only reach that counts.`,
                    coach_r2_protectbut:    `<strong>You protected the relational skills — good — but the mix is very digital-heavy.</strong> At this share even some priority capabilities risk going to a video. Make sure the depth-needing objectives stayed coached, not just nominally “protected.”`,
                    coach_r2_reachoverfit:  `<strong>You pushed consultative-selling training to a €190 self-paced module to hit 2.800 learners.</strong> Selling outcome-based contracts is relational and judgment-heavy. If 2.800 people watch a video and attach rate doesn’t move, you’ve reached everyone and changed nothing — that’s reach over fit.`,
                    coach_r2_alldigital:    `<strong>Maximizing reach by sending everything to cheap digital is the second classic error.</strong> The headcount looks great; the capability doesn’t move. Some objectives need a room and a coach — pick the mode for the objective, not for the cost line.`,
                    coach_r2_underfunded:   `<strong>You barely reallocated.</strong> Leaving most of the EUR 9.6M of inertia spend in place means the alignment score hardly moves and the ~2.800 gap stays open. The freeze isn’t the constraint — your willingness to retire legacy spend is.`,
                    coach_r3_business:      `<strong>Now you’re measuring what the Board asked for.</strong> Behavior change plus leading operational indicators feeding a business-impact index is the move from activity to outcome. Attach rate and mean-time-to-resolution are numbers the CFO already trusts.`,
                    coach_r3_learning:      `<strong>Test scores are a step up from smile sheets, but they stop at Level 2.</strong> The CEO wants to know what business number moves. Knowledge gained is not behavior changed, and behavior changed is not attach rate moved — go one more level.`,
                    coach_r3_activity:      `<strong>92% completion and 4.6 satisfaction — I rejected those in Round 0.</strong> If I cut your budget 30%, what business number gets worse, and can you prove the link? Activity is not impact; this scorecard fails the only test the Board set.`,
                    coach_r3_defensible:    `<strong>That’s a defensible business-impact index.</strong> Baselines, owners and named data sources for each metric — with the weakest causal link disclosed honestly — is exactly what survives a live stress-test. Measuring fewer things well beats a glossy dashboard you can’t defend.`,
                    coach_r3_vague:         `<strong>A causal claim with no baseline or owner is just a hope.</strong> “Training drives attach rate” isn’t a metric until someone owns it and a data source can show the before and after. Tighten the chain or I’ll pull the weakest link in the room.`,
                    coach_r3_nochain:       `<strong>A single headline number with no logic is the easiest thing to dismantle.</strong> I’ll ask one question about how learning caused that index to move, and if there’s no chain behind it, the whole scorecard goes with it.`,
                    coach_r3_underinvest:   `<strong>You can’t prove impact for free.</strong> With near-zero measurement investment you’ll be back defending the redesign with the same completions and smile sheets the CEO already rejected. Decide where the evidence is worth paying for.`,
                    coach_r4_tradeoff:      `<strong>That’s the pitch that holds.</strong> Before/after on all four KPIs, an explicit list of what you retired to fund the gap, and the whole thing inside €14,6M — that’s a case the CFO can defend to the Board, not a vision they have to take on faith.`,
                    coach_r4_vision:        `<strong>A “learning organization” vision with the numbers in the appendix is what the last head pitched.</strong> The Board froze the budget because it stopped believing the story without the numbers. Lead with the KPIs and the trade-off, not the aspiration.`,
                    coach_r4_money:         `<strong>You’ve assumed €18M.</strong> The Board froze you at €14,6M and offered €10,2M if you fail. Asking for more money is what triggered the audit — show me the reallocation inside €14,6M, or you’ve handed me the case for the 30% cut.`,
                    coach_r4_governed:      `<strong>Strong close.</strong> The trade-off pitch plus quarterly realignment checkpoints answers the question that sinks most redesigns: how does this stay aligned when no one’s watching? You’ve made L&D a governance function, not a one-off project.`,
                    coach_r4_oneoff:        `<strong>Beautiful portfolio — for today’s strategy.</strong> Helventer 2030 will shift twice before 2028. With no realignment cadence, your alignment score decays back toward 34% by inertia, and we’re back here in two years. How does this stay aligned when no one’s watching?`,
                    coach_r4_defend:        `<strong>Defending every assumption reads as brittle.</strong> A redesign with no admitted weakness tells me you haven’t pressure-tested it. Concede the weakest causal link openly and the rest of the case gets <em>more</em> credible, not less.`,
                    coach_r4_popularity:    `<strong>“People love these programs” is the answer the last L&D head gave before the audit.</strong> Satisfaction is not strategic alignment and it is not impact. Fall back on popularity under pressure and you’ve confirmed exactly why the budget was frozen.`,

